WEBVTT

00:00.398 --> 00:01.768
- [Instructor] So we have a system of two

00:01.768 --> 00:02.939
linear equations here.

00:02.939 --> 00:06.863
This first equation, X minus
four Y is equal to negative 18,

00:06.863 --> 00:09.544
and the second equation,
negative X plus three Y

00:09.544 --> 00:10.794
is equal to 11.

00:12.374 --> 00:16.373
Now what we're gonna do
is find an X and Y pair

00:16.373 --> 00:19.282
that satisfies both of these equations.

00:19.282 --> 00:22.514
That's what solving the
system actually means.

00:22.514 --> 00:24.267
As you might already have seen,

00:24.267 --> 00:25.881
there's a bunch of X and Y pairs

00:25.881 --> 00:27.338
that satisfy this first equation.

00:27.338 --> 00:28.529
In fact, if you were to graph them,

00:28.529 --> 00:29.747
they would form a line,

00:29.747 --> 00:31.285
and there's a bunch of other X and Y pairs

00:31.285 --> 00:32.639
that satisfy this other equation,

00:32.639 --> 00:33.722
the second equation,

00:33.722 --> 00:34.792
and if you were to graph them,

00:34.792 --> 00:35.941
it would form a line.

00:35.941 --> 00:38.513
And so if you find the X and
Y pair that satisfy both,

00:38.513 --> 00:40.967
that would be the
intersection of the lines,

00:40.967 --> 00:42.458
so let's do that.

00:42.458 --> 00:43.645
So actually, I'm just gonna rewrite

00:43.645 --> 00:45.160
the first equation over here,

00:45.160 --> 00:47.794
so I'm gonna write X minus four Y

00:47.794 --> 00:49.794
is equal to negative 18.

00:51.424 --> 00:53.552
So, we've already seen in algebra

00:53.552 --> 00:55.156
that as long as we do the same thing

00:55.156 --> 00:56.840
to both sides of the equation,

00:56.840 --> 00:59.336
we can maintain our equality.

00:59.336 --> 01:01.441
So what if we were to add,

01:01.441 --> 01:03.885
and our goal here is to
eliminate one of the variables

01:03.885 --> 01:06.331
so we have one equation with one unknown,

01:06.331 --> 01:08.189
so what if we were to add

01:08.189 --> 01:11.779
this negative X plus three Y
to the left hand side here?

01:11.779 --> 01:14.029
So negative X plus three Y,

01:14.940 --> 01:16.147
well, that looks pretty good

01:16.147 --> 01:19.485
because an X and a negative
X are going to cancel out,

01:19.485 --> 01:20.963
and we are going to be left

01:20.963 --> 01:24.547
with negative four Y, plus three Y.

01:24.547 --> 01:28.203
Well, that's just going to be negative Y.

01:28.203 --> 01:30.136
So by adding the left hand side

01:30.136 --> 01:32.059
of this bottom equation
to the left hand side

01:32.059 --> 01:33.016
of the top equation,

01:33.016 --> 01:34.967
we were able to cancel out the Xs.

01:34.967 --> 01:36.706
We had X, and we had a negative X.

01:36.706 --> 01:38.322
That was very nice for us.

01:38.322 --> 01:39.942
So what do we do on the right hand side?

01:39.942 --> 01:41.263
We've already said that we have to add

01:41.263 --> 01:44.143
the same thing to both
sides of an equation.

01:44.143 --> 01:45.726
We might be tempted

01:47.268 --> 01:49.727
to just say, well, if I
have to add the same thing

01:49.727 --> 01:50.560
to both sides,

01:50.560 --> 01:53.229
well, maybe I have to add a negative X

01:53.229 --> 01:56.008
plus three Y to that side.

01:56.008 --> 01:57.496
But that's not going to help us much.

01:57.496 --> 02:00.247
We're gonna have negative
18 minus X plus three Y.

02:00.247 --> 02:02.204
We would have introduced an X

02:02.204 --> 02:04.304
on the right hand side of the equation,

02:04.304 --> 02:05.337
but what if we could add something

02:05.337 --> 02:07.887
that's equivalent to
negative X plus three Y

02:07.887 --> 02:11.724
that does not introduce the X variable?

02:11.724 --> 02:13.820
Well, we know that the number 11

02:13.820 --> 02:16.704
is equivalent to negative X plus three Y.

02:16.704 --> 02:17.802
How do we know that?

02:17.802 --> 02:19.747
Well, that second equation tells us that.

02:19.747 --> 02:22.690
So once again, all I'm doing
is I'm adding the same thing

02:22.690 --> 02:25.616
to both sides of that top equation.

02:25.616 --> 02:28.547
On the left, I'm expressing
it as negative X plus three Y,

02:28.547 --> 02:29.811
but the second equation tells us

02:29.811 --> 02:31.722
that negative X plus three Y

02:31.722 --> 02:33.301
is going to be equal to 11.

02:33.301 --> 02:36.615
It's introducing that second constraint,

02:36.615 --> 02:38.944
and so let's add 11 to
the right hand side,

02:38.944 --> 02:41.109
which is, once again, I
know I keep repeating it,

02:41.109 --> 02:43.400
it's the same thing as
negative X plus three Y.

02:43.400 --> 02:45.143
So negative 18 plus 11

02:45.143 --> 02:47.099
is negative seven,

02:47.099 --> 02:49.596
and since we added the
same thing to both sides,

02:49.596 --> 02:51.738
the equality still holds,

02:51.738 --> 02:55.633
and we get negative Y is
equal to negative seven,

02:55.633 --> 02:58.023
or divide both sides by negative one

02:58.023 --> 03:01.043
or multiply both sides by negative one.

03:01.043 --> 03:03.673
So multiply both sides by negative one.

03:03.673 --> 03:06.552
We get Y is equal to seven,

03:06.552 --> 03:08.985
so we have the Y coordinate

03:08.985 --> 03:12.818
of the X, Y pair that
satisfies both of these.

03:13.750 --> 03:15.695
Now how do we find the X?

03:15.695 --> 03:18.075
Well, we can just substitute
this Y equals seven

03:18.075 --> 03:19.782
to either one of these.

03:19.782 --> 03:22.017
When Y equals seven, we
should get the same X

03:22.017 --> 03:24.710
regardless of which equation we use.

03:24.710 --> 03:26.595
So let's use the top equation.

03:26.595 --> 03:30.086
So we know that X minus four times,

03:30.086 --> 03:31.200
instead of writing Y,

03:31.200 --> 03:32.802
I'm gonna write four times seven

03:32.802 --> 03:34.434
'cause we're gonna figure out what is X

03:34.434 --> 03:35.972
when Y is seven?

03:35.972 --> 03:40.133
That is going to be equal to negative 18,

03:40.133 --> 03:42.223
and so let's see, negative
four or four times seven.

03:42.223 --> 03:43.919
That is 28.

03:43.919 --> 03:45.677
So let's see, I could just solve for X.

03:45.677 --> 03:48.028
I could add 28 to both sides,

03:48.028 --> 03:50.028
so add 28 to both sides.

03:52.585 --> 03:53.652
On the left hand side,

03:53.652 --> 03:55.968
negative 28, positive
28, those cancel out.

03:55.968 --> 03:57.408
I'm just left with an X,

03:57.408 --> 03:58.490
and on the right hand side,

03:58.490 --> 04:01.157
I get negative 18 plus 28 is 10.

04:02.848 --> 04:03.681
So there you have it.

04:03.681 --> 04:07.098
I have the X, Y pair that satisfies both.

04:08.224 --> 04:09.152
X equals 10.

04:09.152 --> 04:10.220
Y equals seven.

04:10.220 --> 04:11.904
I could write it here.

04:11.904 --> 04:13.536
So I could write it as coordinates.

04:13.536 --> 04:16.688
I could write it as 10 comma seven,

04:16.688 --> 04:17.864
and notice, what I just did here,

04:17.864 --> 04:18.968
I encourage you.

04:18.968 --> 04:20.885
Substitute Y equals seven here,

04:20.885 --> 04:23.081
and you would also get X equals 10.

04:23.081 --> 04:26.812
Either way, you would
have come to X equals 10,

04:26.812 --> 04:30.029
and to visualize what is going on here,

04:30.029 --> 04:31.584
let's visualize it really fast.

04:31.584 --> 04:33.913
Let me draw some coordinate axes.

04:33.913 --> 04:37.443
Whoops, I meant to draw a
straighter line than that.

04:37.443 --> 04:38.734
Alright, there you go,

04:38.734 --> 04:41.334
so let's say that is our Y axis,

04:41.334 --> 04:42.334
and that is,

04:45.990 --> 04:48.240
whoops, that is our X axis,

04:50.244 --> 04:51.894
and then, let's see,

04:51.894 --> 04:56.061
the top equation is gonna
look something like this.

04:57.861 --> 05:00.759
It's gonna look something like this,

05:00.759 --> 05:02.417
and then that bottom equation

05:02.417 --> 05:04.834
is gonna look something like,

05:07.597 --> 05:09.710
lemme draw a little bit nicer than that.

05:09.710 --> 05:12.960
It's going to look something like this,

05:13.826 --> 05:15.032
something like that.

05:15.032 --> 05:16.186
Lemme draw that bottom one here

05:16.186 --> 05:19.491
so you see the point of intersection,

05:19.491 --> 05:22.170
and so the point of
intersection right over here,

05:22.170 --> 05:24.359
that is an X, Y pair that satisfies

05:24.359 --> 05:26.022
both of these equations,

05:26.022 --> 05:27.682
and that we just saw,

05:27.682 --> 05:31.101
it happens when X is equal to 10,

05:31.101 --> 05:33.101
and Y is equal to seven.

05:34.941 --> 05:37.173
Once again, this white line,

05:37.173 --> 05:38.845
that's all the X and Y pairs

05:38.845 --> 05:40.755
that satisfy the top equation.

05:40.755 --> 05:43.013
This orange line, that's
all the X and Y pairs

05:43.013 --> 05:45.469
that satisfy the orange equation,

05:45.469 --> 05:46.908
and where they intersect,

05:46.908 --> 05:48.435
that point is on both lines.

05:48.435 --> 05:50.896
It satisfies both equations,

05:50.896 --> 05:52.493
and once again, take X equals 10,

05:52.493 --> 05:53.413
Y equals seven.

05:53.413 --> 05:55.185
Substitute it back into
either one of these,

05:55.185 --> 05:57.852
and you will see that it holds.
